/3) for ZenRayProgress streaming. Root cause confirmed: a video dropped as mkv/avi/mov only wrote a {transcoding:true} placeholder + a line in pages/.zr-transcode-queue.json, but nothing consumed that queue, so it stayed \"transcoding\" forever. I added the missing link: a reusable transcode engine (scripts/video-mse-lib.mjs) and a polling worker (scripts/transcode-worker.mjs). The engine runs native ffmpeg (multi-thread) to produce the project's mse-av format (1 video-only fMP4 H.264 + 1 audio-only fMP4 AAC stereo per language + VTT subtitles), with -g 48 added for denser seek keyframes. It splits each stream into 3MB chunks, NAMES each chunk by its SHA-256 (anonymized/non-sequential), pushes them to the PRIVATE shard (zenray-media-NN) via the safe Git Data API (blobs + base_tree + commit + ref force:false), and writes the mse-av manifest (with repo: shard) separately to zenray-content. The worker polls the queue, resolves the source from a local path (sourcePath field, or same-named file in a --watch folder, default ~/Movies/ZenRayInbox), reports progress via queue status + an HTTP status endpoint on :3010, finalizes the placeholder node into the playable mse-av manifest, and dequeues. Also fixed a latent bug in api/shard.js (it served .vtt as application/octet-stream -> subtitles silently refused; now text/vtt). Did NOT touch the React player/UI (steps 2-3). End-to-end test on a generated 12s MKV (H.264 + FR/JP audio + EN subtitle) passed: transcoded (6 video fragments, 2 audio, 1 VTT), pushed 4 hashed blobs to zenray-media-03, wrote mse-av manifest to zenray-content, finalized placeholder, dequeued; /api/stream returned 206 with a valid fMP4 'ftyp' init box; VTT now served as text/vtt. Test artifacts cleaned from the repos. npm run build passes.\ndocs/video-streaming-report.md:1303:Decisions/constraints honored: token via gh (never committed); private shards via /api/upload-shard active-shard rollover; safe Git Data API (blobs+base_tree+commit+ref force:false), never clone; manifest contract for StreamVideoMSE.jsx unchanged (video.codecs/fragIndex, audio[].codecs, repo field). I kept the existing scripts/video-mse.mjs CLI untouched for zero regression (the new engine is a separate importable module). Hashed chunks are grouped as media/<id>/<stream>/<sha256>.bin so /api/stream needs NO change; the design's deeper ab/cd/<hash>.bin-at-root sharding would require a stream.js edit and was intentionally deferred. RISQUE 1: source resolution is local-path/watch-folder for now; the front uploading the raw source is the next (frontend) step and the worker already supports a source:{repo,dir,parts} shape via resolveSource without changes. One non-blocking 'queue save: conflict' (stale sha during progress writes) is expected and best-effort by design. Per global rule, committed Memory/PROGRESS.md automatically (commit 014f8f1); ZenRayProgress code changes were left uncommitted for the orchestrator. git status shows scripts/video-mse.mjs as modified but that change is from a PRIOR session, not this task — I did not edit it.\ndocs/video-streaming-report.md:1310:Step 2/3 (web import wiring) for ZenRayProgress video streaming. The drop/paste capture flow already existed in src/App.jsx (runCapture -> captureFiles, setCaptureToast, notify with 'video-queued'); I left it untouched per the \"minimize App.jsx changes\" constraint. The real missing links were: (1) the Mac worker had no way to get the source file from a web drag-drop (no disk path), and (2) the placeholder node had no progress UI. I closed both. captureVideoTranscode now uploads the raw source chunked to the PRIVATE shard and records source:{repo,dir,parts} in the queue entry; added a 100MB threshold so oversized mp4/webm also route to local transcode. The worker's resolveSource now downloads that source from the shard (via /api/shard) into /tmp, transcodes, and cleans up the temp file (the absolute-path and --watch-folder modes still work as fallbacks). MediaViewer now renders